================================================================================================
EXPERIMENT B (measurement) — HOW DISCRIMINATIVE IS A SHARED RUN?
================================================================================================

  Queries measured (amount block @ 0.01 non-empty): 30,640
  Candidates surviving amount blocking   mean 14.72   median 1
  Of those, sharing a digit run          mean 6.30   median 1
  Share of surviving candidates that share a run: 42.7661%

  Queries where NO surviving candidate shares a run: 10,447 (34.0960%)
  Queries where exactly one does:                    10,088 (32.9243%)
  Queries where more than one does:                  10,105 (32.9798%)

  Amount blocking has already cut the pool to ~15, so the question is
  whether a shared run narrows it further.

  It does NOT narrow it much: 42.77% of amount-surviving candidates
  share a run with the query. A predicate that keeps well over a third of the
  pool is a weak discriminator at this stage — most of its power was already
  spent by the amount block.

  Critically, on 34.10% of queries NO surviving candidate
  shares a run at all. As a hard filter that forces an abstention on every one of
  them, which caps match rate accordingly — the boost form avoids that by falling
  back to cosine.

  Redundancy check — can the boost change anything at all?
      queries where boost COULD reorder (some but not all candidates share): 6,131
      of those, cosine's top-1 ALREADY shares a run: 6,131 (100.0000%)

      That is 100%. The boost cannot change a single prediction, and not by
      coincidence: the shared digit run IS what the character n-grams are
      scoring on. Cosine has already absorbed this signal, so adding it
      explicitly is redundant.

